---
title: Sorting (Vue) Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Vue examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue refs can be passed directly where the adapter expects reactive table options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sorting-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use sorting features. Adding the sorting feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side sorting, also set up "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // if using client-side sorting\n  // manualSorting: true, // if using manual server-side sorting\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spreading the entire built-in registry ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns: { ...sortFns }"},{"type":"text","value":") still works, but it puts every built-in sorting function in your bundle. Registering just the functions you use, or passing a function directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column option, is recommended. The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn: 'auto'"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alphanumeric"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datetime"},{"type":"text","value":" from the registry based on the column's data type, so register the ones your columns rely on."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sorting-vue-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting (Vue) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table provides solutions for just about any sorting use-case you might have. This guide will walk you through the various options that you can use to customize the built-in client-side sorting functionality, as well as how to opt out of client-side sorting in favor of manual server-side sorting."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sorting-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sorting state is defined as an array of objects with the following shape:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type ColumnSort = {\n  id: string\n  desc: boolean\n}\ntype SortingState = ColumnSort[]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since the sorting state is an array, it is possible to sort by multiple columns at once. Read more about the multi-sorting customizations down "},{"type":"link","href":"#multi-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"below"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"accessing-sorting-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing Sorting State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can read the sorting state from the table instance with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.sorting.get()"},{"type":"text","value":". Because the Vue adapter backs table atoms with Vue refs and computed values, this read is reactive when it happens inside a template, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"computed(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"watch(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" sources, or render functions. Outside of a Vue reactive context, it is a plain snapshot of the current value."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n})\n\ntable.atoms.sorting.get() // reactive inside templates and computed values; a snapshot elsewhere","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"However, if you need access to the sorting state outside of the table, you can \"control\" the sorting state like down below."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"controlled-sorting-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled Sorting State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need easy access to the sorting state in other parts of your application, you can own the sorting state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the sorting value can be used elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side sorting) without depending on the table instance."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([]) // can set initial sorting state here\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom) // a Vue ref\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom, // table sorting APIs now update sortingAtom\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSortingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Pass the current ref value through a getter so the adapter can track it. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const sorting = ref<SortingState>([])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get sorting() {\n      return sorting.value\n    },\n  },\n  onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n    sorting.value =\n      updater instanceof Function ? updater(sorting.value) : updater\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"initial-sorting-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial Sorting State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you do not need to control the sorting state in your own state management or scope, but you still want to set an initial sorting state, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" table option instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'name',\n        desc: true, // sort by name in descending order by default\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not use both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":" at the same time, as the controlled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":" value will override the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting should operate over the same dataset as filtering and pagination. If the server returns only a page or filtered subset, client-side sorting sorts only those loaded rows, not the full dataset."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full decision framework and the cases where mixing client-side and server-side operations is intentional."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client-side sorted row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when sorting inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" options. If sorting is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a sorting state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the sorting change handler when needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-server-side-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Server-Side Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you plan to just use your own server-side sorting in your back-end logic, you do not need to provide a sorted row model. But if you have provided a sorted row model, but you want to disable it, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature }) // feature needed for sorting state/APIs\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the sorting state for your server-side query\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualSorting: true, // use pre-sorted row model instead of sorted row model\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hoisting the sorting state into your own scope (with an external atom or the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSortingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern) is covered in the "},{"type":"link","href":"#controlled-sorting-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled Sorting State"}]},{"type":"text","value":" section above."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" is set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", the table will assume that the data that you provide is already sorted, and will not apply any sorting to it."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To implement client-side sorting, add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" call. Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" and the individual sorting functions you use from TanStack Table:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sorting-rowmodelfns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting RowModelFns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default sorting function for all columns is inferred from the data type of the column. However, it can be useful to define the exact sorting function that you want to use for a specific column, especially if any of your data is nullable or not a standard data type."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can determine a custom sorting function on a per-column basis using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column option."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, there are 6 built-in sorting functions to choose from:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alphanumeric"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values without case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alphanumericCaseSensitive"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values with case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts by text/string values without case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"textCaseSensitive"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts by text/string values with case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datetime"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts by time, use this if your values are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":" objects."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"basic"},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorts using a basic/standard "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a > b ? 1 : a < b ? -1 : 0"},{"type":"text","value":" comparison. This is the fastest sorting function, but may not be the most accurate."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also define your own custom sorting functions, either inline as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column option, or by name in the sorting function registry that you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"custom-sorting-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Sorting Functions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether you register a custom sorting function in the registry passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" or pass it directly as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column option, it should have the following signature:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"//optionally use the SortFn to infer the parameter types\nconst myCustomSortFn: SortFn<TFeatures, TData> = (\n  rowA: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  rowB: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  columnId: string,\n) => {\n  return //-1, 0, or 1 - access any row data using rowA.original and rowB.original\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The comparison function does not need to take whether or not the column is in descending or ascending order into account. The row models will take care of that logic. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" functions only need to provide a consistent comparison."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every sorting function receives 2 rows and a column ID and is expected to compare the two rows using the column ID to return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":" in ascending order. Here's a cheat sheet:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ascending Order"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a < b"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a === b"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a > b"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // use built-in sorting function by name\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn', // reference a custom sorting function registered with createSortedRowModel\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Birthday',\n    accessorKey: 'birthday',\n    sortFn: 'datetime', // recommended for date columns\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Profile',\n    accessorKey: 'profile',\n    // use custom sorting function directly\n    sortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.original.someProperty - rowB.original.someProperty\n    },\n  },\n]\n//...\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n    myCustomSortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) =>\n      rowA.original[columnId] > rowB.original[columnId]\n        ? 1\n        : rowA.original[columnId] < rowB.original[columnId]\n          ? -1\n          : 0,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn'"},{"type":"text","value":" string references to typecheck, register the function in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" slot of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":". When the function is registered there, TypeScript infers the available string keys from the registry, so no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"declare module"},{"type":"text","value":" augmentation is needed. Alternatively, skip the registry entirely by passing the function directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column option."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"customize-sorting-function-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Customize Sorting Function Behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting functions support an optional \"hanging\" property:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn.resolveDataValue"},{"type":"text","value":" - normalizes each row's value before the two sides are compared. It is honored by every sorting function built with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructSortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" helper, which includes all built-in sorting functions."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructSortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" helper builds a sorting function from a value-level comparator ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":") plus that optional resolver. Keeping the comparison in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" and the normalization in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveDataValue"},{"type":"text","value":" means a variant of an existing sorting function only has to swap the resolver. The definition is attached to the returned function, so you can spread any sorting function built with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructSortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" and override only what differs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, a version of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alphanumeric"},{"type":"text","value":" that ignores diacritics, so that \"Éric Bernard\" sorts next to \"Eric Brandon\" instead of after \"Zak O'Sullivan\":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const stripDiacritics = (value: string) =>\n  value.normalize('NFD').replace(/\\p{Diacritic}/gu, '')\n\nconst alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics = constructSortFn({\n  ...sortFn_alphanumeric, // reuse the comparator\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    stripDiacritics(sortFn_alphanumeric.resolveDataValue!(value)),\n})\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same pattern works when defining a new sorting function from scratch:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const byLastName = constructSortFn({\n  sort: (dataValueA, dataValueB) =>\n    dataValueA === dataValueB ? 0 : dataValueA > dataValueB ? 1 : -1,\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    String(value ?? '')\n      .split(' ')\n      .at(-1) ?? '',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"customize-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Customize Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a lot of table and column options that you can use to further customize the sorting UX and behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"disable-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can disable sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" column option or table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'ID',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for this column\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for the entire table\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"sorting-direction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Direction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the first sorting direction when cycling through the sorting for a column using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" APIs is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns. You can change this behavior with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortDescFirst"},{"type":"text","value":" column option or table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortDescFirst: true, //sort by name in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns)\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortDescFirst: false, //sort by age in ascending order first (default is descending for number columns)\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  sortDescFirst: true, //sort by all columns in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may want to explicitly set the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortDescFirst"},{"type":"text","value":" column option on any columns that have nullable values. The table may not be able to properly determine if a column is a number or a string if it contains nullable values."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"invert-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invert Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inverting sorting is not the same as changing the default sorting direction. If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invertSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" column option is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" for a column, then the \"desc/asc\" sorting states will still cycle like normal, but the actual sorting of the rows will be inverted. This is useful for values that have an inverted best/worst scale where lower numbers are better, e.g. a ranking (1st, 2nd, 3rd) or golf-like scoring."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    invertSorting: true, // invert the sorting for this column. 1st -> 2nd -> 3rd -> ... even if \"desc\" sorting is applied\n  },\n  //...\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"sort-undefined-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sort Undefined Values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any undefined values will be sorted to the beginning or end of the list based on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortUndefined"},{"type":"text","value":" column option or table option. You can customize this behavior for your specific use-case."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If not specified, the default value for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortUndefined"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", and undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending), if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first'"},{"type":"text","value":" - Undefined values will be pushed to the beginning of the list"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'last'"},{"type":"text","value":" - Undefined values will be pushed to the end of the list"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" - Undefined values will be passed to the sorting function like any other value with no special handling; the sorting function is responsible for handling them"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"},{"type":"text","value":" - Undefined values will be sorted with higher priority (ascending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the beginning of the list)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":" - Undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list)"}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first'"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'last'"},{"type":"text","value":" options are available in v9."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    sortUndefined: -1, // 'first' | 'last' | 1 | -1 | false\n  },\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"sorting-removal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Removal"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the ability to remove sorting while cycling through the sorting states for a column is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableSortingRemoval"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. This behavior is useful if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default behavior when using either the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getToggleSortingHandler"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" APIs is to cycle through the sorting states like this (the first direction depends on the column's data type and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortDescFirst"},{"type":"text","value":" option, as discussed "},{"type":"link","href":"#sorting-direction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"above"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; a string column is shown here):"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ..."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you disable sorting removal, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none'"},{"type":"text","value":" state is skipped after the first sort:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ..."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once a column is sorted and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableSortingRemoval"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":", toggling the sorting on that column will never remove the sorting. However, if the user sorts by another column and it is not a multi-sort event, then the sorting will be removed from the previous column and just applied to the new column."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableSortingRemoval"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSortingRemoval: false, // disable the ability to remove sorting on columns (sorting can never return to 'none' once applied)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"multi-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting by multiple columns at once is enabled by default if using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getToggleSortingHandler"},{"type":"text","value":" API. If the user holds the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift"},{"type":"text","value":" key while clicking on a column header, the table will sort by that column in addition to the columns that are already sorted. If you use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" API, you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"disable-multi-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable Multi-Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can disable multi-sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableMultiSort"},{"type":"text","value":" column option or table option. Disabling multi-sorting for a specific column will replace all existing sorting with the new column's sorting."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Created At',\n    accessorKey: 'createdAt',\n    enableMultiSort: false, // always sort by just this column if sorting by this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiSort: false, // disable multi-sorting for the entire table\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"customize-multi-sorting-trigger","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Customize Multi-Sorting Trigger"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift"},{"type":"text","value":" key is used to trigger multi-sorting. You can change this behavior with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isMultiSortEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. You can even specify that all sorting events should trigger multi-sorting by returning "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" from the custom function."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => true, // normal click triggers multi-sorting\n  //or\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => e.ctrlKey || e.shiftKey, // also use the `Ctrl` key to trigger multi-sorting\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"multi-sorting-limit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Sorting Limit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, there is no limit to the number of columns that can be sorted at once. You can set a limit using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxMultiSortColCount"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  maxMultiSortColCount: 3, // only allow 3 columns to be sorted at once\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"multi-sorting-removal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Sorting Removal"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the ability to remove multi-sorts is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableMultiRemove"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiRemove: false, // disable the ability to remove multi-sorts\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reset-sorting-when-data-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset Sorting When Data Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting state is preserved when the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" option changes by default. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetSorting: true"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset sorting whenever a new data reference is processed. The reset restores "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":", or an empty sorting state when no initial value was provided."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This option responds only to data changes. Changing sorting, filters, or grouping does not trigger it. The global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":" option overrides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" when explicitly set."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be careful when combining this option with manual/server-side sorting: a server response normally replaces "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", so enabling the reset can immediately clear the sorting state that requested that response."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sorting-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a lot of sorting related APIs that you can use to hook up to your UI or other logic. Here is a list of all of the sorting APIs and some of their use-cases."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" - Set the sorting state directly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" - Reset the sorting state to the initial state or clear it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanSort"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for enabling/disabling the sorting UI for a column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsSorted"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for showing a visual sorting indicator for a column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getToggleSortingHandler"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. Add to a sort arrow (icon button), menu item, or simply the entire column header cell. This handler will call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" with the correct parameters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. If using instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getToggleSortingHandler"},{"type":"text","value":", you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.clearSorting"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for a \"clear sorting\" button or menu item for a specific column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getNextSortingOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by next. (asc/desc/clear in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getFirstSortDir"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by first. (asc/desc in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAutoSortDir"},{"type":"text","value":" - Determines whether the first sorting direction will be ascending or descending for a column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAutoSortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" - Used internally to find the default sorting function for a column if none is specified."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" - Returns the exact sorting function being used for a column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanMultiSort"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for enabling/disabling the multi-sorting UI for a column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSortIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" - Useful for showing a badge or indicator of the column's sort order in a multi-sort scenario. i.e. whether or not it is the first, second, third, etc. column to be sorted."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"sorting-setup","text":"Sorting Setup","level":3},{"id":"sorting-vue-guide","text":"Sorting (Vue) Guide","level":2},{"id":"sorting-state","text":"Sorting State","level":3},{"id":"accessing-sorting-state","text":"Accessing Sorting State","level":4},{"id":"controlled-sorting-state","text":"Controlled Sorting State","level":4},{"id":"initial-sorting-state","text":"Initial Sorting State","level":4},{"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-sorting","text":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Sorting","level":3},{"id":"manual-server-side-sorting","text":"Manual Server-Side Sorting","level":3},{"id":"client-side-sorting","text":"Client-Side Sorting","level":3},{"id":"sorting-rowmodelfns","text":"Sorting RowModelFns","level":3},{"id":"custom-sorting-functions","text":"Custom Sorting Functions","level":4},{"id":"customize-sorting-function-behavior","text":"Customize Sorting Function Behavior","level":4},{"id":"customize-sorting","text":"Customize Sorting","level":3},{"id":"disable-sorting","text":"Disable Sorting","level":4},{"id":"sorting-direction","text":"Sorting Direction","level":4},{"id":"invert-sorting","text":"Invert Sorting","level":4},{"id":"sort-undefined-values","text":"Sort Undefined Values","level":4},{"id":"sorting-removal","text":"Sorting Removal","level":4},{"id":"multi-sorting","text":"Multi-Sorting","level":4},{"id":"disable-multi-sorting","text":"Disable Multi-Sorting","level":5},{"id":"customize-multi-sorting-trigger","text":"Customize Multi-Sorting Trigger","level":5},{"id":"multi-sorting-limit","text":"Multi-Sorting Limit","level":5},{"id":"multi-sorting-removal","text":"Multi-Sorting Removal","level":5},{"id":"reset-sorting-when-data-changes","text":"Reset Sorting When Data Changes","level":3},{"id":"sorting-apis","text":"Sorting APIs","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Vue examples:\n\n- [Sorting](../examples/sorting)\n\nVue refs can be passed directly where the adapter expects reactive table options.\n\n### Sorting Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use sorting features. Adding the sorting feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side sorting, also set up `sortedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // if using client-side sorting\n  // manualSorting: true, // if using manual server-side sorting\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Spreading the entire built-in registry (`sortFns: { ...sortFns }`) still works, but it puts every built-in sorting function in your bundle. Registering just the functions you use, or passing a function directly to the `sortFn` column option, is recommended. The default `sortFn: 'auto'` resolves to `alphanumeric`, `text`, or `datetime` from the registry based on the column's data type, so register the ones your columns rely on.\n\n## Sorting (Vue) Guide\n\nTanStack Table provides solutions for just about any sorting use-case you might have. This guide will walk you through the various options that you can use to customize the built-in client-side sorting functionality, as well as how to opt out of client-side sorting in favor of manual server-side sorting.\n\n### Sorting State\n\nThe sorting state is defined as an array of objects with the following shape:\n\n```ts\ntype ColumnSort = {\n  id: string\n  desc: boolean\n}\ntype SortingState = ColumnSort[]\n```\n\nSince the sorting state is an array, it is possible to sort by multiple columns at once. Read more about the multi-sorting customizations down [below](#multi-sorting).\n\n#### Accessing Sorting State\n\nYou can read the sorting state from the table instance with `table.atoms.sorting.get()`. Because the Vue adapter backs table atoms with Vue refs and computed values, this read is reactive when it happens inside a template, `computed(...)`, `watch(...)` sources, or render functions. Outside of a Vue reactive context, it is a plain snapshot of the current value.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n})\n\ntable.atoms.sorting.get() // reactive inside templates and computed values; a snapshot elsewhere\n```\n\nHowever, if you need access to the sorting state outside of the table, you can \"control\" the sorting state like down below.\n\n#### Controlled Sorting State\n\nIf you need easy access to the sorting state in other parts of your application, you can own the sorting state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the sorting value can be used elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side sorting) without depending on the table instance.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([]) // can set initial sorting state here\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom) // a Vue ref\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom, // table sorting APIs now update sortingAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.sorting` plus `onSortingChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Pass the current ref value through a getter so the adapter can track it. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst sorting = ref<SortingState>([])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get sorting() {\n      return sorting.value\n    },\n  },\n  onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n    sorting.value =\n      updater instanceof Function ? updater(sorting.value) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\n#### Initial Sorting State\n\nIf you do not need to control the sorting state in your own state management or scope, but you still want to set an initial sorting state, you can use the `initialState` table option instead of `state`.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'name',\n        desc: true, // sort by name in descending order by default\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not use both `initialState.sorting` and `state.sorting` at the same time, as the controlled `state.sorting` value will override the `initialState.sorting`.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Sorting\n\nSorting should operate over the same dataset as filtering and pagination. If the server returns only a page or filtered subset, client-side sorting sorts only those loaded rows, not the full dataset.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework and the cases where mixing client-side and server-side operations is intentional.\n\nThe client-side sorted row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when sorting inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetAll`, and `manualPagination` options. If sorting is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a sorting state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the sorting change handler when needed.\n\n### Manual Server-Side Sorting\n\nIf you plan to just use your own server-side sorting in your back-end logic, you do not need to provide a sorted row model. But if you have provided a sorted row model, but you want to disable it, you can use the `manualSorting` table option.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature }) // feature needed for sorting state/APIs\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the sorting state for your server-side query\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualSorting: true, // use pre-sorted row model instead of sorted row model\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nHoisting the sorting state into your own scope (with an external atom or the `state.sorting` plus `onSortingChange` pattern) is covered in the [Controlled Sorting State](#controlled-sorting-state) section above.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When `manualSorting` is set to `true`, the table will assume that the data that you provide is already sorted, and will not apply any sorting to it.\n\n### Client-Side Sorting\n\nTo implement client-side sorting, add the `rowSortingFeature`, `sortedRowModel`, and `sortFns` to your `tableFeatures` call. Import `createSortedRowModel` and the individual sorting functions you use from TanStack Table:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Sorting RowModelFns\n\nThe default sorting function for all columns is inferred from the data type of the column. However, it can be useful to define the exact sorting function that you want to use for a specific column, especially if any of your data is nullable or not a standard data type.\n\nYou can determine a custom sorting function on a per-column basis using the `sortFn` column option.\n\nBy default, there are 6 built-in sorting functions to choose from:\n\n- `alphanumeric` - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values without case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `alphanumericCaseSensitive` - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values with case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `text` - Sorts by text/string values without case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `textCaseSensitive` - Sorts by text/string values with case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `datetime` - Sorts by time, use this if your values are `Date` objects.\n- `basic` - Sorts using a basic/standard `a > b ? 1 : a < b ? -1 : 0` comparison. This is the fastest sorting function, but may not be the most accurate.\n\nYou can also define your own custom sorting functions, either inline as the `sortFn` column option, or by name in the sorting function registry that you pass to `createSortedRowModel`.\n\n#### Custom Sorting Functions\n\nWhether you register a custom sorting function in the registry passed to `createSortedRowModel` or pass it directly as a `sortFn` column option, it should have the following signature:\n\n```ts\n//optionally use the SortFn to infer the parameter types\nconst myCustomSortFn: SortFn<TFeatures, TData> = (\n  rowA: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  rowB: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  columnId: string,\n) => {\n  return //-1, 0, or 1 - access any row data using rowA.original and rowB.original\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The comparison function does not need to take whether or not the column is in descending or ascending order into account. The row models will take care of that logic. `sortFn` functions only need to provide a consistent comparison.\n\nEvery sorting function receives 2 rows and a column ID and is expected to compare the two rows using the column ID to return `-1`, `0`, or `1` in ascending order. Here's a cheat sheet:\n\n| Return | Ascending Order |\n| ------ | --------------- |\n| `-1`   | `a < b`         |\n| `0`    | `a === b`       |\n| `1`    | `a > b`         |\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // use built-in sorting function by name\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn', // reference a custom sorting function registered with createSortedRowModel\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Birthday',\n    accessorKey: 'birthday',\n    sortFn: 'datetime', // recommended for date columns\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Profile',\n    accessorKey: 'profile',\n    // use custom sorting function directly\n    sortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.original.someProperty - rowB.original.someProperty\n    },\n  },\n]\n//...\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n    myCustomSortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) =>\n      rowA.original[columnId] > rowB.original[columnId]\n        ? 1\n        : rowA.original[columnId] < rowB.original[columnId]\n          ? -1\n          : 0,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> **TypeScript Note:** For `sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn'` string references to typecheck, register the function in the `sortFns` slot of `tableFeatures`. When the function is registered there, TypeScript infers the available string keys from the registry, so no `declare module` augmentation is needed. Alternatively, skip the registry entirely by passing the function directly to the `sortFn` column option.\n\n#### Customize Sorting Function Behavior\n\nSorting functions support an optional \"hanging\" property:\n\n- `sortFn.resolveDataValue` - normalizes each row's value before the two sides are compared. It is honored by every sorting function built with the `constructSortFn` helper, which includes all built-in sorting functions.\n\nThe `constructSortFn` helper builds a sorting function from a value-level comparator (`sort`) plus that optional resolver. Keeping the comparison in `sort` and the normalization in `resolveDataValue` means a variant of an existing sorting function only has to swap the resolver. The definition is attached to the returned function, so you can spread any sorting function built with `constructSortFn` and override only what differs.\n\nFor example, a version of `alphanumeric` that ignores diacritics, so that \"Éric Bernard\" sorts next to \"Eric Brandon\" instead of after \"Zak O'Sullivan\":\n\n```ts\nconst stripDiacritics = (value: string) =>\n  value.normalize('NFD').replace(/\\p{Diacritic}/gu, '')\n\nconst alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics = constructSortFn({\n  ...sortFn_alphanumeric, // reuse the comparator\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    stripDiacritics(sortFn_alphanumeric.resolveDataValue!(value)),\n})\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics },\n})\n```\n\nThe same pattern works when defining a new sorting function from scratch:\n\n```ts\nconst byLastName = constructSortFn({\n  sort: (dataValueA, dataValueB) =>\n    dataValueA === dataValueB ? 0 : dataValueA > dataValueB ? 1 : -1,\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    String(value ?? '')\n      .split(' ')\n      .at(-1) ?? '',\n})\n```\n\n### Customize Sorting\n\nThere are a lot of table and column options that you can use to further customize the sorting UX and behavior.\n\n#### Disable Sorting\n\nYou can disable sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the `enableSorting` column option or table option.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'ID',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for this column\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for the entire table\n})\n```\n\n#### Sorting Direction\n\nBy default, the first sorting direction when cycling through the sorting for a column using the `toggleSorting` APIs is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns. You can change this behavior with the `sortDescFirst` column option or table option.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortDescFirst: true, //sort by name in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns)\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortDescFirst: false, //sort by age in ascending order first (default is descending for number columns)\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  sortDescFirst: true, //sort by all columns in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns)\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> You may want to explicitly set the `sortDescFirst` column option on any columns that have nullable values. The table may not be able to properly determine if a column is a number or a string if it contains nullable values.\n\n#### Invert Sorting\n\nInverting sorting is not the same as changing the default sorting direction. If `invertSorting` column option is `true` for a column, then the \"desc/asc\" sorting states will still cycle like normal, but the actual sorting of the rows will be inverted. This is useful for values that have an inverted best/worst scale where lower numbers are better, e.g. a ranking (1st, 2nd, 3rd) or golf-like scoring.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    invertSorting: true, // invert the sorting for this column. 1st -> 2nd -> 3rd -> ... even if \"desc\" sorting is applied\n  },\n  //...\n]\n```\n\n#### Sort Undefined Values\n\nAny undefined values will be sorted to the beginning or end of the list based on the `sortUndefined` column option or table option. You can customize this behavior for your specific use-case.\n\nIf not specified, the default value for `sortUndefined` is `1`, and undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending), if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list.\n\n- `'first'` - Undefined values will be pushed to the beginning of the list\n- `'last'` - Undefined values will be pushed to the end of the list\n- `false` - Undefined values will be passed to the sorting function like any other value with no special handling; the sorting function is responsible for handling them\n- `-1` - Undefined values will be sorted with higher priority (ascending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the beginning of the list)\n- `1` - Undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `'first'` and `'last'` options are available in v9.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    sortUndefined: -1, // 'first' | 'last' | 1 | -1 | false\n  },\n]\n```\n\n#### Sorting Removal\n\nBy default, the ability to remove sorting while cycling through the sorting states for a column is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the `enableSortingRemoval` table option. This behavior is useful if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted.\n\nThe default behavior when using either the `getToggleSortingHandler` or `toggleSorting` APIs is to cycle through the sorting states like this (the first direction depends on the column's data type and the `sortDescFirst` option, as discussed [above](#sorting-direction); a string column is shown here):\n\n`'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ...`\n\nIf you disable sorting removal, the `'none'` state is skipped after the first sort:\n\n`'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ...`\n\nOnce a column is sorted and `enableSortingRemoval` is `false`, toggling the sorting on that column will never remove the sorting. However, if the user sorts by another column and it is not a multi-sort event, then the sorting will be removed from the previous column and just applied to the new column.\n\n> Set `enableSortingRemoval` to `false` if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSortingRemoval: false, // disable the ability to remove sorting on columns (sorting can never return to 'none' once applied)\n})\n```\n\n#### Multi-Sorting\n\nSorting by multiple columns at once is enabled by default if using the `column.getToggleSortingHandler` API. If the user holds the `Shift` key while clicking on a column header, the table will sort by that column in addition to the columns that are already sorted. If you use the `column.toggleSorting` API, you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. (`column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)`).\n\n##### Disable Multi-Sorting\n\nYou can disable multi-sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the `enableMultiSort` column option or table option. Disabling multi-sorting for a specific column will replace all existing sorting with the new column's sorting.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Created At',\n    accessorKey: 'createdAt',\n    enableMultiSort: false, // always sort by just this column if sorting by this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiSort: false, // disable multi-sorting for the entire table\n})\n```\n\n##### Customize Multi-Sorting Trigger\n\nBy default, the `Shift` key is used to trigger multi-sorting. You can change this behavior with the `isMultiSortEvent` table option. You can even specify that all sorting events should trigger multi-sorting by returning `true` from the custom function.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => true, // normal click triggers multi-sorting\n  //or\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => e.ctrlKey || e.shiftKey, // also use the `Ctrl` key to trigger multi-sorting\n})\n```\n\n##### Multi-Sorting Limit\n\nBy default, there is no limit to the number of columns that can be sorted at once. You can set a limit using the `maxMultiSortColCount` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  maxMultiSortColCount: 3, // only allow 3 columns to be sorted at once\n})\n```\n\n##### Multi-Sorting Removal\n\nBy default, the ability to remove multi-sorts is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the `enableMultiRemove` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiRemove: false, // disable the ability to remove multi-sorts\n})\n```\n\n### Reset Sorting When Data Changes\n\nSorting state is preserved when the `data` option changes by default. Set `autoResetSorting: true` to reset sorting whenever a new data reference is processed. The reset restores `initialState.sorting`, or an empty sorting state when no initial value was provided.\n\nThis option responds only to data changes. Changing sorting, filters, or grouping does not trigger it. The global `autoResetAll` option overrides `autoResetSorting` when explicitly set.\n\nBe careful when combining this option with manual/server-side sorting: a server response normally replaces `data`, so enabling the reset can immediately clear the sorting state that requested that response.\n\n### Sorting APIs\n\nThere are a lot of sorting related APIs that you can use to hook up to your UI or other logic. Here is a list of all of the sorting APIs and some of their use-cases.\n\n- `table.setSorting` - Set the sorting state directly.\n- `table.resetSorting` - Reset the sorting state to the initial state or clear it.\n\n- `column.getCanSort` - Useful for enabling/disabling the sorting UI for a column.\n- `column.getIsSorted` - Useful for showing a visual sorting indicator for a column.\n\n- `column.getToggleSortingHandler` - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. Add to a sort arrow (icon button), menu item, or simply the entire column header cell. This handler will call `column.toggleSorting` with the correct parameters.\n- `column.toggleSorting` - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. If using instead of `column.getToggleSortingHandler`, you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. (`column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)`)\n- `column.clearSorting` - Useful for a \"clear sorting\" button or menu item for a specific column.\n\n- `column.getNextSortingOrder` - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by next. (asc/desc/clear in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)\n- `column.getFirstSortDir` - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by first. (asc/desc in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)\n- `column.getAutoSortDir` - Determines whether the first sorting direction will be ascending or descending for a column.\n- `column.getAutoSortFn` - Used internally to find the default sorting function for a column if none is specified.\n- `column.getSortFn` - Returns the exact sorting function being used for a column.\n\n- `column.getCanMultiSort` - Useful for enabling/disabling the multi-sorting UI for a column.\n- `column.getSortIndex` - Useful for showing a badge or indicator of the column's sort order in a multi-sort scenario. i.e. whether or not it is the first, second, third, etc. column to be sorted.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Sorting (Vue) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Vue examples:\n\n- [Sorting](../examples/sorting)\n\nVue refs can be passed directly where the adapter expects reactive table options.\n\n### Sorting Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use sorting features. Adding the sorting feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side sorting, also set up `sortedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // if using client-side sorting\n  // manualSorting: true, // if using manual server-side sorting\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Spreading the entire built-in registry (`sortFns: { ...sortFns }`) still works, but it puts every built-in sorting function in your bundle. Registering just the functions you use, or passing a function directly to the `sortFn` column option, is recommended. The default `sortFn: 'auto'` resolves to `alphanumeric`, `text`, or `datetime` from the registry based on the column's data type, so register the ones your columns rely on.\n\n## Sorting (Vue) Guide\n\nTanStack Table provides solutions for just about any sorting use-case you might have. This guide will walk you through the various options that you can use to customize the built-in client-side sorting functionality, as well as how to opt out of client-side sorting in favor of manual server-side sorting.\n\n### Sorting State\n\nThe sorting state is defined as an array of objects with the following shape:\n\n```ts\ntype ColumnSort = {\n  id: string\n  desc: boolean\n}\ntype SortingState = ColumnSort[]\n```\n\nSince the sorting state is an array, it is possible to sort by multiple columns at once. Read more about the multi-sorting customizations down [below](#multi-sorting).\n\n#### Accessing Sorting State\n\nYou can read the sorting state from the table instance with `table.atoms.sorting.get()`. Because the Vue adapter backs table atoms with Vue refs and computed values, this read is reactive when it happens inside a template, `computed(...)`, `watch(...)` sources, or render functions. Outside of a Vue reactive context, it is a plain snapshot of the current value.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n})\n\ntable.atoms.sorting.get() // reactive inside templates and computed values; a snapshot elsewhere\n```\n\nHowever, if you need access to the sorting state outside of the table, you can \"control\" the sorting state like down below.\n\n#### Controlled Sorting State\n\nIf you need easy access to the sorting state in other parts of your application, you can own the sorting state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the sorting value can be used elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side sorting) without depending on the table instance.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([]) // can set initial sorting state here\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom) // a Vue ref\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom, // table sorting APIs now update sortingAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.sorting` plus `onSortingChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Pass the current ref value through a getter so the adapter can track it. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst sorting = ref<SortingState>([])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get sorting() {\n      return sorting.value\n    },\n  },\n  onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n    sorting.value =\n      updater instanceof Function ? updater(sorting.value) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\n#### Initial Sorting State\n\nIf you do not need to control the sorting state in your own state management or scope, but you still want to set an initial sorting state, you can use the `initialState` table option instead of `state`.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'name',\n        desc: true, // sort by name in descending order by default\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not use both `initialState.sorting` and `state.sorting` at the same time, as the controlled `state.sorting` value will override the `initialState.sorting`.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Sorting\n\nSorting should operate over the same dataset as filtering and pagination. If the server returns only a page or filtered subset, client-side sorting sorts only those loaded rows, not the full dataset.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework and the cases where mixing client-side and server-side operations is intentional.\n\nThe client-side sorted row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when sorting inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetAll`, and `manualPagination` options. If sorting is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a sorting state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the sorting change handler when needed.\n\n### Manual Server-Side Sorting\n\nIf you plan to just use your own server-side sorting in your back-end logic, you do not need to provide a sorted row model. But if you have provided a sorted row model, but you want to disable it, you can use the `manualSorting` table option.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/vue-store'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature }) // feature needed for sorting state/APIs\n\nconst sortingAtom = createAtom<SortingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the sorting state for your server-side query\nconst sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualSorting: true, // use pre-sorted row model instead of sorted row model\n  atoms: {\n    sorting: sortingAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nHoisting the sorting state into your own scope (with an external atom or the `state.sorting` plus `onSortingChange` pattern) is covered in the [Controlled Sorting State](#controlled-sorting-state) section above.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When `manualSorting` is set to `true`, the table will assume that the data that you provide is already sorted, and will not apply any sorting to it.\n\n### Client-Side Sorting\n\nTo implement client-side sorting, add the `rowSortingFeature`, `sortedRowModel`, and `sortFns` to your `tableFeatures` call. Import `createSortedRowModel` and the individual sorting functions you use from TanStack Table:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n  sortFn_datetime,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    text: sortFn_text,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Sorting RowModelFns\n\nThe default sorting function for all columns is inferred from the data type of the column. However, it can be useful to define the exact sorting function that you want to use for a specific column, especially if any of your data is nullable or not a standard data type.\n\nYou can determine a custom sorting function on a per-column basis using the `sortFn` column option.\n\nBy default, there are 6 built-in sorting functions to choose from:\n\n- `alphanumeric` - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values without case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `alphanumericCaseSensitive` - Sorts by mixed alphanumeric values with case-sensitivity. Slower, but more accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `text` - Sorts by text/string values without case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `textCaseSensitive` - Sorts by text/string values with case-sensitivity. Faster, but less accurate if your strings contain numbers that need to be naturally sorted.\n- `datetime` - Sorts by time, use this if your values are `Date` objects.\n- `basic` - Sorts using a basic/standard `a > b ? 1 : a < b ? -1 : 0` comparison. This is the fastest sorting function, but may not be the most accurate.\n\nYou can also define your own custom sorting functions, either inline as the `sortFn` column option, or by name in the sorting function registry that you pass to `createSortedRowModel`.\n\n#### Custom Sorting Functions\n\nWhether you register a custom sorting function in the registry passed to `createSortedRowModel` or pass it directly as a `sortFn` column option, it should have the following signature:\n\n```ts\n//optionally use the SortFn to infer the parameter types\nconst myCustomSortFn: SortFn<TFeatures, TData> = (\n  rowA: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  rowB: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  columnId: string,\n) => {\n  return //-1, 0, or 1 - access any row data using rowA.original and rowB.original\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The comparison function does not need to take whether or not the column is in descending or ascending order into account. The row models will take care of that logic. `sortFn` functions only need to provide a consistent comparison.\n\nEvery sorting function receives 2 rows and a column ID and is expected to compare the two rows using the column ID to return `-1`, `0`, or `1` in ascending order. Here's a cheat sheet:\n\n| Return | Ascending Order |\n| ------ | --------------- |\n| `-1`   | `a < b`         |\n| `0`    | `a === b`       |\n| `1`    | `a > b`         |\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // use built-in sorting function by name\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn', // reference a custom sorting function registered with createSortedRowModel\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Birthday',\n    accessorKey: 'birthday',\n    sortFn: 'datetime', // recommended for date columns\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Profile',\n    accessorKey: 'profile',\n    // use custom sorting function directly\n    sortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) => {\n      return rowA.original.someProperty - rowB.original.someProperty\n    },\n  },\n]\n//...\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: {\n    alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric,\n    datetime: sortFn_datetime,\n    myCustomSortFn: (rowA, rowB, columnId) =>\n      rowA.original[columnId] > rowB.original[columnId]\n        ? 1\n        : rowA.original[columnId] < rowB.original[columnId]\n          ? -1\n          : 0,\n  },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> **TypeScript Note:** For `sortFn: 'myCustomSortFn'` string references to typecheck, register the function in the `sortFns` slot of `tableFeatures`. When the function is registered there, TypeScript infers the available string keys from the registry, so no `declare module` augmentation is needed. Alternatively, skip the registry entirely by passing the function directly to the `sortFn` column option.\n\n#### Customize Sorting Function Behavior\n\nSorting functions support an optional \"hanging\" property:\n\n- `sortFn.resolveDataValue` - normalizes each row's value before the two sides are compared. It is honored by every sorting function built with the `constructSortFn` helper, which includes all built-in sorting functions.\n\nThe `constructSortFn` helper builds a sorting function from a value-level comparator (`sort`) plus that optional resolver. Keeping the comparison in `sort` and the normalization in `resolveDataValue` means a variant of an existing sorting function only has to swap the resolver. The definition is attached to the returned function, so you can spread any sorting function built with `constructSortFn` and override only what differs.\n\nFor example, a version of `alphanumeric` that ignores diacritics, so that \"Éric Bernard\" sorts next to \"Eric Brandon\" instead of after \"Zak O'Sullivan\":\n\n```ts\nconst stripDiacritics = (value: string) =>\n  value.normalize('NFD').replace(/\\p{Diacritic}/gu, '')\n\nconst alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics = constructSortFn({\n  ...sortFn_alphanumeric, // reuse the comparator\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    stripDiacritics(sortFn_alphanumeric.resolveDataValue!(value)),\n})\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, alphanumericIgnoreDiacritics },\n})\n```\n\nThe same pattern works when defining a new sorting function from scratch:\n\n```ts\nconst byLastName = constructSortFn({\n  sort: (dataValueA, dataValueB) =>\n    dataValueA === dataValueB ? 0 : dataValueA > dataValueB ? 1 : -1,\n  resolveDataValue: (value) =>\n    String(value ?? '')\n      .split(' ')\n      .at(-1) ?? '',\n})\n```\n\n### Customize Sorting\n\nThere are a lot of table and column options that you can use to further customize the sorting UX and behavior.\n\n#### Disable Sorting\n\nYou can disable sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the `enableSorting` column option or table option.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'ID',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for this column\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSorting: false, // disable sorting for the entire table\n})\n```\n\n#### Sorting Direction\n\nBy default, the first sorting direction when cycling through the sorting for a column using the `toggleSorting` APIs is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns. You can change this behavior with the `sortDescFirst` column option or table option.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortDescFirst: true, //sort by name in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns)\n  },\n  {\n    header: () => 'Age',\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    sortDescFirst: false, //sort by age in ascending order first (default is descending for number columns)\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  sortDescFirst: true, //sort by all columns in descending order first (default is ascending for string columns and descending for number columns)\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> You may want to explicitly set the `sortDescFirst` column option on any columns that have nullable values. The table may not be able to properly determine if a column is a number or a string if it contains nullable values.\n\n#### Invert Sorting\n\nInverting sorting is not the same as changing the default sorting direction. If `invertSorting` column option is `true` for a column, then the \"desc/asc\" sorting states will still cycle like normal, but the actual sorting of the rows will be inverted. This is useful for values that have an inverted best/worst scale where lower numbers are better, e.g. a ranking (1st, 2nd, 3rd) or golf-like scoring.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    invertSorting: true, // invert the sorting for this column. 1st -> 2nd -> 3rd -> ... even if \"desc\" sorting is applied\n  },\n  //...\n]\n```\n\n#### Sort Undefined Values\n\nAny undefined values will be sorted to the beginning or end of the list based on the `sortUndefined` column option or table option. You can customize this behavior for your specific use-case.\n\nIf not specified, the default value for `sortUndefined` is `1`, and undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending), if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list.\n\n- `'first'` - Undefined values will be pushed to the beginning of the list\n- `'last'` - Undefined values will be pushed to the end of the list\n- `false` - Undefined values will be passed to the sorting function like any other value with no special handling; the sorting function is responsible for handling them\n- `-1` - Undefined values will be sorted with higher priority (ascending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the beginning of the list)\n- `1` - Undefined values will be sorted with lower priority (descending) (if ascending, undefined will appear on the end of the list)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `'first'` and `'last'` options are available in v9.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Rank',\n    accessorKey: 'rank',\n    sortUndefined: -1, // 'first' | 'last' | 1 | -1 | false\n  },\n]\n```\n\n#### Sorting Removal\n\nBy default, the ability to remove sorting while cycling through the sorting states for a column is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the `enableSortingRemoval` table option. This behavior is useful if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted.\n\nThe default behavior when using either the `getToggleSortingHandler` or `toggleSorting` APIs is to cycle through the sorting states like this (the first direction depends on the column's data type and the `sortDescFirst` option, as discussed [above](#sorting-direction); a string column is shown here):\n\n`'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ...`\n\nIf you disable sorting removal, the `'none'` state is skipped after the first sort:\n\n`'none' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> 'asc' -> 'desc' -> ...`\n\nOnce a column is sorted and `enableSortingRemoval` is `false`, toggling the sorting on that column will never remove the sorting. However, if the user sorts by another column and it is not a multi-sort event, then the sorting will be removed from the previous column and just applied to the new column.\n\n> Set `enableSortingRemoval` to `false` if you want to ensure that at least one column is always sorted.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableSortingRemoval: false, // disable the ability to remove sorting on columns (sorting can never return to 'none' once applied)\n})\n```\n\n#### Multi-Sorting\n\nSorting by multiple columns at once is enabled by default if using the `column.getToggleSortingHandler` API. If the user holds the `Shift` key while clicking on a column header, the table will sort by that column in addition to the columns that are already sorted. If you use the `column.toggleSorting` API, you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. (`column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)`).\n\n##### Disable Multi-Sorting\n\nYou can disable multi-sorting for either a specific column or the entire table using the `enableMultiSort` column option or table option. Disabling multi-sorting for a specific column will replace all existing sorting with the new column's sorting.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Created At',\n    accessorKey: 'createdAt',\n    enableMultiSort: false, // always sort by just this column if sorting by this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiSort: false, // disable multi-sorting for the entire table\n})\n```\n\n##### Customize Multi-Sorting Trigger\n\nBy default, the `Shift` key is used to trigger multi-sorting. You can change this behavior with the `isMultiSortEvent` table option. You can even specify that all sorting events should trigger multi-sorting by returning `true` from the custom function.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => true, // normal click triggers multi-sorting\n  //or\n  isMultiSortEvent: (e) => e.ctrlKey || e.shiftKey, // also use the `Ctrl` key to trigger multi-sorting\n})\n```\n\n##### Multi-Sorting Limit\n\nBy default, there is no limit to the number of columns that can be sorted at once. You can set a limit using the `maxMultiSortColCount` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  maxMultiSortColCount: 3, // only allow 3 columns to be sorted at once\n})\n```\n\n##### Multi-Sorting Removal\n\nBy default, the ability to remove multi-sorts is enabled. You can disable this behavior using the `enableMultiRemove` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableMultiRemove: false, // disable the ability to remove multi-sorts\n})\n```\n\n### Reset Sorting When Data Changes\n\nSorting state is preserved when the `data` option changes by default. Set `autoResetSorting: true` to reset sorting whenever a new data reference is processed. The reset restores `initialState.sorting`, or an empty sorting state when no initial value was provided.\n\nThis option responds only to data changes. Changing sorting, filters, or grouping does not trigger it. The global `autoResetAll` option overrides `autoResetSorting` when explicitly set.\n\nBe careful when combining this option with manual/server-side sorting: a server response normally replaces `data`, so enabling the reset can immediately clear the sorting state that requested that response.\n\n### Sorting APIs\n\nThere are a lot of sorting related APIs that you can use to hook up to your UI or other logic. Here is a list of all of the sorting APIs and some of their use-cases.\n\n- `table.setSorting` - Set the sorting state directly.\n- `table.resetSorting` - Reset the sorting state to the initial state or clear it.\n\n- `column.getCanSort` - Useful for enabling/disabling the sorting UI for a column.\n- `column.getIsSorted` - Useful for showing a visual sorting indicator for a column.\n\n- `column.getToggleSortingHandler` - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. Add to a sort arrow (icon button), menu item, or simply the entire column header cell. This handler will call `column.toggleSorting` with the correct parameters.\n- `column.toggleSorting` - Useful for hooking up the sorting UI for a column. If using instead of `column.getToggleSortingHandler`, you have to manually pass in whether or not to use multi-sorting. (`column.toggleSorting(desc, multi)`)\n- `column.clearSorting` - Useful for a \"clear sorting\" button or menu item for a specific column.\n\n- `column.getNextSortingOrder` - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by next. (asc/desc/clear in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)\n- `column.getFirstSortDir` - Useful for showing which direction the column will sort by first. (asc/desc in a tooltip/menu item/aria-label or something)\n- `column.getAutoSortDir` - Determines whether the first sorting direction will be ascending or descending for a column.\n- `column.getAutoSortFn` - Used internally to find the default sorting function for a column if none is specified.\n- `column.getSortFn` - Returns the exact sorting function being used for a column.\n\n- `column.getCanMultiSort` - Useful for enabling/disabling the multi-sorting UI for a column.\n- `column.getSortIndex` - Useful for showing a badge or indicator of the column's sort order in a multi-sort scenario. i.e. whether or not it is the first, second, third, etc. column to be sorted.\n"}
